WEBVTT

00:00:00.402 --> 00:00:03.969
Most software asks you to trust it.

00:00:04.734 --> 00:00:07.840
This section is the opposite: every claim

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:11.056
Lolly makes about your content, your data

00:00:11.149 --> 00:00:14.118
and its own behaviour is written down here

00:00:14.178 --> 00:00:17.399
with the mechanism that enforces it, so

00:00:17.465 --> 00:00:20.195
you can check rather than believe.

00:00:20.993 --> 00:00:24.167
Three questions bring people here.

00:00:24.953 --> 00:00:27.594
Start wherever yours sits.

00:00:28.731 --> 00:00:31.893
Where did this content come from?

00:00:32.651 --> 00:00:35.778
Every image, PDF and video Lolly renders

00:00:35.852 --> 00:00:39.148
carries Content Credentials by default - a

00:00:39.246 --> 00:00:42.300
signed record of what made it, from what,

00:00:42.358 --> 00:00:43.620
and when.

00:00:44.388 --> 00:00:47.249
When a file arrives with a history already

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:50.336
attached, Lolly preserves it rather than

00:00:50.426 --> 00:00:53.518
flattening it, so the chain stays intact

00:00:53.598 --> 00:00:55.194
through an edit.

00:00:55.954 --> 00:00:58.639
Content Credentials - what gets signed,

00:00:58.699 --> 00:01:01.144
what the signature proves, and who the

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:02.719
signer is.

00:01:03.474 --> 00:01:06.009
Content Credentials - Engineering - the

00:01:06.082 --> 00:01:09.995
C2PA implementation itself: manifests,

00:01:10.057 --> 00:01:13.220
ingredients, action histories, formats.

00:01:13.992 --> 00:01:17.590
Our AI Stance - where AI is welcome, where

00:01:17.653 --> 00:01:21.088
it is not, and why AI-generated content

00:01:21.129 --> 00:01:22.770
declares itself.

00:01:23.891 --> 00:01:27.093
Can I check that for myself?

00:01:27.877 --> 00:01:30.305
You can, and you should - the point of

00:01:30.384 --> 00:01:33.071
provenance is that it does not need our

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:34.194
word.

00:01:34.978 --> 00:01:37.923
Verify It Yourself - walk the claims on

00:01:37.984 --> 00:01:41.193
this site against a real export, step by

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:42.294
step.

00:01:43.074 --> 00:01:46.340
Security & Verification - how the code is

00:01:46.415 --> 00:01:48.869
built, signed and checked.

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:52.498
Threat Model - what Lolly defends against,

00:01:52.563 --> 00:01:55.594
and what it explicitly does not.

00:01:56.353 --> 00:01:58.968
Parser Inventory - every format Lolly

00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:01.577
parses, because parsers are where

00:02:01.682 --> 00:02:04.869
untrusted input meets your device.

00:02:05.665 --> 00:02:08.616
Server Surface - the complete list of what

00:02:08.665 --> 00:02:10.869
a server ever sees.

00:02:12.007 --> 00:02:15.043
What happens to my data?

00:02:15.851 --> 00:02:18.993
Lolly renders on your device.

00:02:19.768 --> 00:02:22.870
That is not a privacy posture bolted on

00:02:22.972 --> 00:02:26.590
afterwards - it is the architecture, and

00:02:26.659 --> 00:02:29.836
it is why most of the usual questions have

00:02:29.928 --> 00:02:31.569
short answers.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.271
Privacy Policy - what is collected, what

00:02:35.329 --> 00:02:38.544
is not, and what leaves your device.

00:02:39.314 --> 00:02:42.172
Data Transfer - moving your work in and

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:44.969
out, with nothing held hostage.

00:02:45.742 --> 00:02:48.513
Inclusive Design - who the software is

00:02:48.575 --> 00:02:50.519
built to work for.

00:02:51.268 --> 00:02:54.259
If you find a place where these pages and

00:02:54.291 --> 00:02:57.860
the code disagree, the code is the bug -

00:02:57.919 --> 00:03:00.508
and the repository is public so you can

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:02.520
prove it either way.
